    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 3476862721,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-cc7dda13693c",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1332.0,
      "y": -177.75,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 385.0,
      "seed": 905732,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Kafka in front\nSources write only to Kafka, and Kafka keeps\neach record for days. If a later step stops,\nthe records wait in Kafka and nothing is lost.\nA buffer with short retention cannot do this.\nIf it keeps events for only one day, every\nlonger outage loses data.\n\nFor example, suppose the topics keep records\nfor 7 days, unless a partition reaches 1 GB\nfirst. Then the sink, the connector that\nwrites Kafka records into Iceberg tables,\nstops for 3 days. When it restarts, it reads\nthe 3 missed days and loses nothing. A buffer\nthat keeps events for 1 day would have deleted\nthe first 2 days before anyone read them.\n\nKafka also keeps the sources simple. A source\nneeds only Kafka credentials to send its data.\nOn the write side, only the sink connectors\nhold S3 and catalog credentials, and only they\nwrite data files and request commits.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-cc7dda13693c",
      "originalText": "Kafka in front\nSources write only to Kafka, and Kafka keeps each record for days. If a later step stops, the records wait in Kafka and nothing is lost. A buffer with short retention cannot do this. If it keeps events for only one day, every longer outage loses data.\n\nFor example, suppose the topics keep records for 7 days, unless a partition reaches 1 GB first. Then the sink, the connector that writes Kafka records into Iceberg tables, stops for 3 days. When it restarts, it reads the 3 missed days and loses nothing. A buffer that keeps events for 1 day would have deleted the first 2 days before anyone read them.\n\nKafka also keeps the sources simple. A source needs only Kafka credentials to send its data. On the write side, only the sink connectors hold S3 and catalog credentials, and only they write data files and request commits.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "kafka-in-front"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 7310206852,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "node-d7627527c13c",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1320.0,
