    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 6974516818,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-9e3fa7d4b2d0",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1094.4,
      "y": 467.5,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 109.19999999999999,
      "height": 1942.5,
      "seed": 372148,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Kafka\nConnect\nKafka\nConnect is a\nservice that\nmoves data\ninto and out\nof Kafka\nwithout\ncustom code.\nIt runs as\nits own set\nof\nprocesses,\ncalled\nworkers,\nnext to the\nKafka\nbrokers. Its\nonly job is\nto run\nconnectors.\n\nA connector\nis a\nready-made\nplugin plus\na configurat\nion. A\nsource\nconnector\nreads an\noutside\nsystem and\nwrites to\nKafka. A\nsink\nconnector\nreads Kafka\ntopics and\nwrites to an\noutside\nsystem.\n\"Sink\" only\nmeans the\nplace where\ndata flows\nto.\n\nKafka\nConnect\nsplits each\nconnector\ninto tasks\nthat work in\nparallel,\nand it\nspreads the\ntasks across\nits workers.\nIf a worker\nstops, the\nother\nworkers take\nover its\ntasks. For\nexample, a\nsink set to\ntasks.max =\n3 that reads\na topic with\n3 partitions\nruns 3\ntasks, and\neach task\nreads one\npartition. A\nfourth task\nwould have\nno partition\nto read.\n\nIn this\npattern,\nboth ends\nuse Kafka\nConnect. The\nCDC source\nconnector\nruns in a\nKafka\nConnect\nservice near\nthe\ndatabase.\nThe Iceberg\nsink\nconnector\nruns in a\nKafka\nConnect\nservice in\nthe cloud.\nWhen this\nmap says\n\"the sink\",\nit means the\nIceberg sink\nconnector\nand its\ntasks.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-9e3fa7d4b2d0",
      "originalText": "Kafka Connect\nKafka Connect is a service that moves data into and out of Kafka without custom code. It runs as its own set of processes, called workers, next to the Kafka brokers. Its only job is to run connectors.\n\nA connector is a ready-made plugin plus a configuration. A source connector reads an outside system and writes to Kafka. A sink connector reads Kafka topics and writes to an outside system. \"Sink\" only means the place where data flows to.\n\nKafka Connect splits each connector into tasks that work in parallel, and it spreads the tasks across its workers. If a worker stops, the other workers take over its tasks. For example, a sink set to tasks.max = 3 that reads a topic with 3 partitions runs 3 tasks, and each task reads one partition. A fourth task would have no partition to read.\n\nIn this pattern, both ends use Kafka Connect. The CDC source connector runs in a Kafka Connect service near the database. The Iceberg sink connector runs in a Kafka Connect service in the cloud. When this map says \"the sink\", it means the Iceberg sink connector and its tasks.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "kafka-connect"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 2056269547,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "node-9d47f2c40db9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1082.4,
      "y": 549.0,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "#F8FAFC",
      "width": 276.0,
      "height": 41.5,
      "seed": 999050,
      "groupIds": [],
