    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 5389408302,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-9d47f2c40db9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1094.4,
      "y": 549.0,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 252.0,
      "height": 875.0,
      "seed": 875896,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Single message transform\n(SMT)\nA single message transform\n(SMT) is a small step that\nKafka Connect runs on each\nrecord. It runs as the record\npasses through a connector. A\nsource connector runs its\ntransforms before the record\nreaches Kafka. A sink\nconnector runs them after it\nreads the record from Kafka,\nand before it writes.\n\nA connector's configuration\nlists its transforms in\norder. Each transform changes\none record at a time: it can\nadd, remove, or rename\nfields, change the record's\ntopic, or drop the record. It\ncannot join or count records,\nbecause it sees only one\nrecord. A later transform can\nuse a field that an earlier\ntransform added, so the order\nmatters.\n\nFor example, the sink unwraps\nthe record from the [CDC\nsource\nconnector](#cdc-outputs) with\nDebezium's\nExtractNewRecordState\ntransform. The envelope for\nthe down event becomes one\nplain row: id 7001, truck\n104, start 08:00, end 09:30.\nThe transform also adds the\noperation and source details\nas columns, such as __op = u\nand __source_ts_ms. Two more\ntransforms can then add a\nnamespace, which is a named\ngroup of tables in the\ncatalog, and join it with the\nsource table name. The result\nnames the target table, so\none sink can route records to\nmany tables.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-9d47f2c40db9",
      "originalText": "Single message transform (SMT)\nA single message transform (SMT) is a small step that Kafka Connect runs on each record. It runs as the record passes through a connector. A source connector runs its transforms before the record reaches Kafka. A sink connector runs them after it reads the record from Kafka, and before it writes.\n\nA connector's configuration lists its transforms in order. Each transform changes one record at a time: it can add, remove, or rename fields, change the record's topic, or drop the record. It cannot join or count records, because it sees only one record. A later transform can use a field that an earlier transform added, so the order matters.\n\nFor example, the sink unwraps the record from the [CDC source connector](#cdc-outputs) with Debezium's ExtractNewRecordState transform. The envelope for the down event becomes one plain row: id 7001, truck 104, start 08:00, end 09:30. The transform also adds the operation and source details as columns, such as __op = u and __source_ts_ms. Two more transforms can then add a namespace, which is a named group of tables in the catalog, and join it with the source table name. The result names the target table, so one sink can route records to many tables.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "single-message-transform"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 4707049561,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "node-209c77b42a5a",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1082.4,
      "y": 630.5,
