      }
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 3527523246,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-b44c8e2d8508",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1094.4,
      "y": 712.0,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 117.60000000000002,
      "height": 1137.5,
      "seed": 272010,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Iceberg commit\nAn Iceberg\ncommit\npublishes new\ndata files as\nthe table's\nnext snapshot.\nA snapshot is\nthe complete\nlist of files\nthat form the\ntable at one\nmoment.\n\nA commit has\ntwo halves.\nFirst, the\nwriter writes\nthe data\nfiles, plus\nmanifest files\nthat list\nthem. Then it\nasks the\ncatalog to\ncommit. A REST\ncatalog such\nas Snowflake\nOpen Catalog\nwrites the new\ntable metadata\nfile itself.\nThen it moves\nthe table's\npointer to\nthat file in\none atomic\nstep.\n\nA reader sees\nall of a\ncommit or none\nof it. Before\nthe commit,\nthe new files\nsit in S3, but\nno reader can\nsee them. So\nin this\npattern, the\ncommit is the\nmoment rows\njoin the\ntable. The\n[Iceberg map](\n../../iceberg/\niceberg.md)\ndescribes the\nfiles, and the\nsink's [commit\ncycle](#sink-u\nnder-the-hood)\nshows when\ncommits\nhappen.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-b44c8e2d8508",
      "originalText": "Iceberg commit\nAn Iceberg commit publishes new data files as the table's next snapshot. A snapshot is the complete list of files that form the table at one moment.\n\nA commit has two halves. First, the writer writes the data files, plus manifest files that list them. Then it asks the catalog to commit. A REST catalog such as Snowflake Open Catalog writes the new table metadata file itself. Then it moves the table's pointer to that file in one atomic step.\n\nA reader sees all of a commit or none of it. Before the commit, the new files sit in S3, but no reader can see them. So in this pattern, the commit is the moment rows join the table. The [Iceberg map](../../iceberg/iceberg.md) describes the files, and the sink's [commit cycle](#sink-under-the-hood) shows when commits happen.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "iceberg-commit"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
