    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 4729621132,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-9947d5412704",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1094.4,
      "y": 875.0,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 151.20000000000002,
      "height": 1015.0,
      "seed": 997437,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Credential vending\nCredential vending\nmeans that the\ncatalog hands out\nshort-lived\nstorage\ncredentials\ntogether with a\ntable. The\ncredentials are\nscoped to that\ntable's location\nin S3. So a\ntrusted reader\nneeds no storage\nrole of its own.\nTable access is\nthen managed in\none place: the\ncatalog's roles. A\nwriter can use\nvended credentials\ntoo, or keep its\nown storage keys.\n\nIn Snowflake, the\nobject that\nconnects to an\nexternal catalog\nis a catalog\nintegration. It\nturns this on with\nACCESS_DELEGATION_\nMODE = VENDED_CRED\nENTIALS. The\ndefault is EXTERNA\nL_VOLUME_CREDENTIA\nLS, which needs an\nexternal volume\ninstead. An\nexternal volume is\na Snowflake\nobject. It names\nthe S3 location\nand an IAM (AWS\nIdentity and\nAccess Management)\nrole that\nSnowflake assumes\nto read it. The\n[catalog\nintegration refere\nnce](https://docs.\nsnowflake.com/en/s\nql-reference/sql/c\nreate-catalog-inte\ngration-rest)\nlists both modes.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-9947d5412704",
      "originalText": "Credential vending\nCredential vending means that the catalog hands out short-lived storage credentials together with a table. The credentials are scoped to that table's location in S3. So a trusted reader needs no storage role of its own. Table access is then managed in one place: the catalog's roles. A writer can use vended credentials too, or keep its own storage keys.\n\nIn Snowflake, the object that connects to an external catalog is a catalog integration. It turns this on with ACCESS_DELEGATION_MODE = VENDED_CREDENTIALS. The default is EXTERNAL_VOLUME_CREDENTIALS, which needs an external volume instead. An external volume is a Snowflake object. It names the S3 location and an IAM (AWS Identity and Access Management) role that Snowflake assumes to read it. The [catalog integration reference](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/sql-reference/sql/create-catalog-integration-rest) lists both modes.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "credential-vending"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 7274526749,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "node-a36087821b39",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
