    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 5170637019,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-773265b8d445",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1932.4,
      "y": 1201.0,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 117.60000000000002,
      "height": 1505.0,
      "seed": 38881,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Under the hood\nThe connector\nruns in a\nKafka Connect\nservice on a\nserver near\nthe database,\ninside the\nsource\nnetwork. It\nconnects out\nto the cloud\nKafka over\nTLS, an\nencrypted\nconnection.\n\nOn its first\nstart, the\nconnector\nreads every\nrow of every\ncaptured table\nonce, as a\nsnapshot. Then\nit follows the\nchange tables.\nIn a test, a\ntable of 2\nmillion rows\ntook about 1\nminute 50\nseconds to\nsnapshot.\n\nThe connector\nstores its\nposition in a\nKafka topic,\nso after a\nrestart it\ncontinues\nwhere it\nstopped. This\nworks only\nwhile SQL\nServer still\nholds the\nmissed\nchanges. By\ndefault, SQL\nServer deletes\nchange rows\nafter 3 days.\nIf the\nconnector\nstays down\nlonger, the\nchanges in\nbetween are\nlost. Then the\ntables must be\nread again in\nfull. [Kafka\nin front](#kaf\nka-in-front)\ncannot help\nhere, because\nthose changes\nnever reached\nKafka.\n\nCDC does not\nfollow column\nchanges. A\ncolumn added\nlater is not\ncaptured until\nan\nadministrator\nrecreates that\ntable's\ncapture\ninstance,\nwhich is its\nCDC setup.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-773265b8d445",
      "originalText": "Under the hood\nThe connector runs in a Kafka Connect service on a server near the database, inside the source network. It connects out to the cloud Kafka over TLS, an encrypted connection.\n\nOn its first start, the connector reads every row of every captured table once, as a snapshot. Then it follows the change tables. In a test, a table of 2 million rows took about 1 minute 50 seconds to snapshot.\n\nThe connector stores its position in a Kafka topic, so after a restart it continues where it stopped. This works only while SQL Server still holds the missed changes. By default, SQL Server deletes change rows after 3 days. If the connector stays down longer, the changes in between are lost. Then the tables must be read again in full. [Kafka in front](#kafka-in-front) cannot help here, because those changes never reached Kafka.\n\nCDC does not follow column changes. A column added later is not captured until an administrator recreates that table's capture instance, which is its CDC setup.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "cdc-under-the-hood"
      }
    },
