      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Under the hood\nSnowflake can\nbind to the\ncatalog in two\nways. This\ntable shows\neach one as it\nis usually set\nup:\n\n| |\nCatalog-linked\ndatabase | One\nIceberg table\nper source\ntable |\n| --- | --- |\n--- |\n| Snowflake\nobjects | A\ncatalog\nintegration\nand one\ndatabase | A\ncatalog\nintegration,\nan external\nvolume, and\none CREATE\nICEBERG TABLE\nper table |\n| A new table\nappears | On\nthe next poll\nafter the sink\ncreates it |\nOnly after the\nsink creates\nit and someone\nregisters it |\n| New commits\nappear | On\nthe next poll\n| Only with\nAUTO_REFRESH\non, or after a\nmanual refresh\n|\n| S3 access |\nVended\ncredentials |\nThe external\nvolume's IAM\nrole |\n\nIn an ordinary\ndatabase,\nCREATE ICEBERG\nTABLE does not\ncreate a table\nin the\ncatalog. It\nregisters a\ntable that the\ncatalog\nalready has,\nunder a\nSnowflake\nname. Until\nthe sink has\ncreated that\ntable, the\nstatement\nfails with\n\"table not\nfound in\ncatalog\". So\nthe per-table\nway needs two\nsteps. First,\nbuild the\ncatalog\nintegration,\nthe external\nvolume, and\nthe database.\nThen register\neach table\nafter its\nfirst record\nhas arrived. A\nsource that\nsends no\nrecords never\ngets a table,\nso its\nregistration\nkeeps failing.\n\nFor a table of\nits own,\nAUTO_REFRESH\nis off by\ndefault, as\nthe [CREATE\nICEBERG TABLE \nreference](htt\nps://docs.snow\nflake.com/en/s\nql-reference/s\nql/create-iceb\nerg-table-rest\n) shows. Then\nSnowflake\nnever polls\nthe catalog\nfor that\ntable. The\ntable keeps an\nold snapshot\nwhile the sink\nkeeps\ncommitting,\nand nothing\nreports an\nerror.\n\nFor example,\nsuppose the\nsink creates a\nnew table at\n10:00 and\ncommits every\n5 minutes.\nWith a\ncatalog-linked\ndatabase, the\ntable appears\nin Snowflake\nby about\n10:00:30. Each\nlater commit\nfollows within\na minute or\nso. With one\ntable per\nsource table,\nthe table\nappears only\nafter someone\nregisters it\nin Snowflake.\nIf\nAUTO_REFRESH\nis off, it\nthen keeps the\nsnapshot it\nfound, while\nthe sink\ncommits 288\ntimes a day.\n\nThe per-table\nway is older\nand more\nmanual, but\nSnowflake does\nnot call it\ndeprecated.\nThe\ncatalog-linked\ndatabase\nremoves the\nper-table\nsetup and the\ntwo-step\norder. It has \n[limits](https\n://docs.snowfl\nake.com/en/use\nr-guide/tables\n-iceberg-catal\nog-linked-data\nbase#considera\ntions-for-usin\ng-a-catalog-li\nnked-database-\nfor-iceberg-ta\nbles): it\nworks only\nwith an\nIceberg REST\ncatalog, and\nit supports no\nreplication,\ncloning, or\nUNDROP.\nRenaming a\ntable in the\ncatalog drops\nand recreates\nit in\nSnowflake. As\nof November\n2026, one\nSnowflake\nreference page\nstill labels\nthe feature as\npreview.\n\nChoose a\ncatalog-linked\ndatabase when\nyou want every\ntable in a\nnamespace,\nkept current\nwith no\ntable-by-table\nwork. Choose\nthe per-table\nway when you\nwant only some\nof the tables,\nor your own\nnames in an\nexisting\ndatabase. It\nalso fits when\nyou need a\nfeature that\nthe linked\ndatabase\nlacks.\n\nEither way,\nSnowflake does\nnot copy the\ncatalog's\npermissions.\nGrant SELECT\non future\nIceberg tables\nin each\nschema, so\nthat new\ntables are\nreadable when\nthey appear.\nIn a\ncatalog-linked\ndatabase, a\ngrant on a\nschema fails\nuntil the\nfirst sync has\ncreated that\nschema.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-60e574fe7d6e",
      "originalText": "Under the hood\nSnowflake can bind to the catalog in two ways. This table shows each one as it is usually set up:\n\n| | Catalog-linked database | One Iceberg table per source table |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Snowflake objects | A catalog integration and one database | A catalog integration, an external volume, and one CREATE ICEBERG TABLE per table |\n| A new table appears | On the next poll after the sink creates it | Only after the sink creates it and someone registers it |\n| New commits appear | On the next poll | Only with AUTO_REFRESH on, or after a manual refresh |\n| S3 access | Vended credentials | The external volume's IAM role |\n\nIn an ordinary database, CREATE ICEBERG TABLE does not create a table in the catalog. It registers a table that the catalog already has, under a Snowflake name. Until the sink has created that table, the statement fails with \"table not found in catalog\". So the per-table way needs two steps. First, build the catalog integration, the external volume, and the database. Then register each table after its first record has arrived. A source that sends no records never gets a table, so its registration keeps failing.\n\nFor a table of its own, AUTO_REFRESH is off by default, as the [CREATE ICEBERG TABLE reference](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/sql-reference/sql/create-iceberg-table-rest) shows. Then Snowflake never polls the catalog for that table. The table keeps an old snapshot while the sink keeps committing, and nothing reports an error.\n\nFor example, suppose the sink creates a new table at 10:00 and commits every 5 minutes. With a catalog-linked database, the table appears in Snowflake by about 10:00:30. Each later commit follows within a minute or so. With one table per source table, the table appears only after someone registers it in Snowflake. If AUTO_REFRESH is off, it then keeps the snapshot it found, while the sink commits 288 times a day.\n\nThe per-table way is older and more manual, but Snowflake does not call it deprecated. The catalog-linked database removes the per-table setup and the two-step order. It has [limits](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/tables-iceberg-catalog-linked-database#considerations-for-using-a-catalog-linked-database-for-iceberg-tables): it works only with an Iceberg REST catalog, and it supports no replication, cloning, or UNDROP. Renaming a table in the catalog drops and recreates it in Snowflake. As of November 2026, one Snowflake reference page still labels the feature as preview.\n\nChoose a catalog-linked database when you want every table in a namespace, kept current with no table-by-table work. Choose the per-table way when you want only some of the tables, or your own names in an existing database. It also fits when you need a feature that the linked database lacks.\n\nEither way, Snowflake does not copy the catalog's permissions. Grant SELECT on future Iceberg tables in each schema, so that new tables are readable when they appear. In a catalog-linked database, a grant on a schema fails until the first sync has created that schema.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "snowflake-under-the-hood"
      }
    },
